How temperature affects responses
In generative models, temperature controls how deterministic vs. creative the output is. With a low temperature (e.g., 0.1–0.3), the model strongly prefers the highest-probability tokens, so the responses are more focused, repeatable, and “safe”. This is ideal for tasks that require accuracy and consistency, such as answering technical questions from documentation or executing instructions. With a higher temperature (e.g., 0.7–1.0), the model samples more from lower-probability options, making responses more diverse and creative, but also more variable and occasionally less precise.

How top_p (nucleus sampling) affects responses
top_p controls how much of the probability mass we allow the model to sample from. Instead of using a fixed temperature only, top_p chooses the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability is at most p (for example, 0.9). When top_p is low, the model samples only from a narrow set of very likely tokens, making answers more conservative and predictable. A higher top_p lets the model consider a larger set of possible tokens and increases diversity. In practice, temperature and top_p are usually tuned together for the desired behavior. For this project, we choose relatively low-to-moderate values so the model stays grounded in the knowledge base while still generating natural-sounding language.
